\title{Validating Item-Level Contamination Detection:\\A Semi-Synthetic Framework with Cross-Subject Matching}

\author{Anonymous}

\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Item-level contamination detection for Large Language Model (LLM) benchmarks remains difficult to validate because the standard ground truth (web-overlap and n-gram labels) measures global exposure rather than differential item behavior.
We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework that injects known differential contamination into real response matrices and measures detection power via $\Delta$TPR.
A cross-subject matching strategy, which conditions on scores from unrelated benchmark domains, eliminates false-positive inflation entirely ($\Delta$FPR$=0.000$ across all tested subjects), while within-subject matching inflates FPR by up to $+0.186$.
We demonstrate portability across benchmarks (MMLU $\Delta$TPR$=0.35$ at 50\% exploitation; GSM8K $\Delta$TPR$=0.82$).
As a co-primary contribution, we provide an empirical ground truth meta-critique: DIF flags and GSM1K contamination gaps are negatively correlated ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$, $N=33$), confirming that web-overlap labels and item-level behavioral change measure fundamentally different constructs.
We motivate the framework through temporal Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis of 5,227 models across 12,508 MMLU items, finding 17.9\%--31.3\% large DIF across six benchmarks, with controls ruling out statistical artifacts (permutation $p<0.001$, random-split 0.18\%).
We report extensive transparency analyses (Simpson's paradox decomposition, Item-Focused Trees, IRT fit diagnostics) and negative results (bifactor model failure, ensemble underperformance) to support informed evaluation of this research direction.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

When we compare LLM benchmark scores across model generations, we implicitly assume temporal comparability: that the same item measures the same construct for both early and late models.
If items function differently across generations, after controlling for overall ability, score comparisons become unreliable.
Psychometrics provides a mature tool for testing this assumption: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis \citep{holland1988differential,mantel1959statistical}.

Several item-level contamination detectors have been proposed for LLM benchmarks, including perturbation-based methods \citep{golchin2024constat}, generation-variance approaches \citep{dong2024dvd}, and membership inference methods \citep{shi2024mink}.
All of these methods share a validation blind spot: they evaluate against web-overlap or n-gram labels that capture whether an item is publicly accessible, not whether an item's difficulty changed differentially across model groups.
This mismatch is not merely theoretical.
Our empirical analysis shows that DIF flags and Li et al.'s web-overlap labels are statistically independent (enrichment$=0.96$, $\chi^2 p=0.258$), and that DIF flags \emph{anti-correlate} with GSM1K contamination gaps ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$).

We propose a semi-synthetic validation framework to address this gap.
The core idea is simple: inject known differential contamination into real response matrices by flipping incorrect$\to$correct responses for designated ``contaminated'' models on designated items, then measure the resulting increase in detection power ($\Delta$TPR).
A key innovation is cross-subject matching: using total scores on \emph{other} benchmark subjects as the MH conditioning variable, which eliminates FPR inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=0.000$) because the matching variable is unaffected by the injection.

As a motivating case study, we apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), finding that 31.3\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C classification) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts.
This signal is not a statistical artifact (permutation $z=388.5$, $p<0.001$; random-split control: 0.18\%), and the pattern replicates across six benchmarks (17.9\%--31.3\%).
However, the interpretation of this signal, whether it reflects item instability, capability improvement, or a combination, remains contested.
Our transparency analyses (Simpson's paradox decomposition, Item-Focused Trees) suggest that item discrimination is the proximal driver, and we report this ambiguity honestly.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A \textbf{semi-synthetic validation framework} for item-level contamination detectors, with cross-subject matching that achieves $\Delta$FPR$=0.000$.
Portable across benchmarks (demonstrated on MMLU and GSM8K).
\item An \textbf{empirical ground truth meta-critique} showing that web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched for evaluating item-level differential detectors (enrichment$\approx 1.0$; GSM1K $r=-0.478$).
\item \textbf{Temporal DIF case study} across six benchmarks (17.9\%--31.3\% large DIF), with extensive transparency analyses and negative results.
\item A \textbf{proposed audit workflow} for benchmark maintainers, with decision rules grounded in the empirical findings.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}
Methods for detecting data contamination in LLM benchmarks span several paradigms.
Perturbation-based approaches, such as ConStat \citep{golchin2024constat}, rephrase items and measure performance drops.
Generation-variance methods like DVD \citep{dong2024dvd} examine response distribution bimodality.
Membership inference approaches, including Min-K\% \citep{shi2024mink}, estimate training set membership from output probabilities.
All these methods validate against web-overlap or n-gram labels, which we argue is a fundamental mismatch for item-level differential detection.

\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}
DIF analysis has been a cornerstone of educational and psychological testing since \citet{mantel1959statistical} introduced the Mantel-Haenszel procedure and \citet{holland1988differential} systematized the ETS classification framework.
Recent work has applied DIF to LLM evaluation \citep{marcelino2024llm}, but no prior study has operated at the scale of METABENCH (5,227 models) or proposed a semi-synthetic validation framework.

\paragraph{Benchmark integrity.}
Growing concerns about benchmark contamination have led to parallel test construction \citep{zhang2024gsm1k}, temporal trend analysis, and calls for benchmark renewal.
Our work complements these efforts by providing a validation infrastructure for the detectors themselves.


\section{Method}

\subsection{MH-DIF Analysis}

The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure tests whether an item functions differently across two groups (reference vs.\ focal) after conditioning on total score.
For each score stratum $k$, a $2 \times 2$ table of correct/incorrect $\times$ group is constructed.
The MH odds ratio $\alpha_{\text{MH}}$ and its log-transform $\Delta_{\text{MH}} = -2.35 \ln(\alpha_{\text{MH}})$ quantify DIF magnitude.
Items are classified per the ETS scheme: A (negligible, $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}|<1.0$), B (moderate, $1.0 \le |\Delta_{\text{MH}}|<1.5$), and C (large, $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}|\ge 1.5$ and significant at $p<0.05$).

We apply MH-DIF with temporal cohort definitions (pre-2024 vs.\ 2024+ models as reference vs.\ focal groups) and validate with ability-based and random-split controls.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}

To measure whether a DIF detector can identify known differential contamination, we inject contamination into real response matrices:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Select $K$ items as ``contaminated.''
\item For each focal-group model with probability $p$ (the exploitation rate), flip incorrect$\to$correct on contaminated items.
\item Run MH-DIF on the modified matrix.
\item Compute $\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$ and $\Delta$FPR $=$ FPR$(p)$ $-$ FPR$(0)$.
\end{enumerate}

The baseline TPR at $p=0$ captures pre-existing DIF; $\Delta$TPR isolates the detection power attributable to the injected signal.

\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}

Standard MH conditions on total score within the same subject.
When contamination is injected into subject $s$, this matching variable is itself contaminated, inflating FPR.
Cross-subject matching conditions on total score across \emph{all other subjects} $S \setminus \{s\}$, keeping the matching variable clean.
This strategy applies to any multi-domain benchmark.


\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Temporal DIF Case Study}
\label{sec:temporal}

\paragraph{MMLU DIF analysis.}
We apply MH-DIF to METABENCH MMLU (5,219 models $\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohorts (reference: pre-2024, $N_\text{ref}=1{,}253$; focal: 2024+, $N_\text{foc}=656$).
Table~\ref{tab:cohort} reports DIF classification by cohort definition.
Temporal DIF yields 31.3\% ETS C items, compared with 2.9\% for ability-quintile cohorts and 0.18\% for random-split control.

