\paragraph{Architecture stratification.}
METABENCH MMLU is 99.98\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).
Excluding the single non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0 with full-population DIF flags).
IRT fit diagnostics are essentially identical: RMSEA=0.082 (full) vs.\ 0.081 (decoder-only), CFI=0.74 (both), $|L_z|>2$ at 98.0\% vs.\ 97.8\%.
Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue; IRT misfit reflects MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality, not population composition.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

\paragraph{MMLU injection study.}
Table~\ref{tab:semisyn} reports detection power under known contamination injection.
At exploitation rate $p=0$ (no injection), baseline TPR is 0.326 and FPR is 0.314, reflecting pre-existing DIF.
As $p$ increases, TPR rises monotonically ($\Delta$TPR$=0.35$ at $p=0.5$, $0.62$ at $p=1.0$), demonstrating dose-response sensitivity.
The critical result: cross-subject matching yields $\Delta$FPR$=0.000$ at all exploitation rates, eliminating false-positive inflation entirely.
In contrast, within-subject matching inflates FPR by $+0.186$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: DIF detection power under known contamination injection on MMLU. Cross-subject matching achieves $\Delta$FPR$=0.000$ at all exploitation rates.}
\label{tab:semisyn}
\small
\begin{tabular}{crrrr}
\toprule
Rate $p$ & TPR & $\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\Delta$FPR \\
\midrule
0.0 & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\
0.3 & 0.450 & 0.124 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\
0.5 & 0.676 & 0.350 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\
0.7 & 0.780 & 0.454 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\
1.0 & 0.950 & 0.624 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We note that $\Delta$TPR$=0.35$ at $p=0.5$ means roughly half of contaminated items at half exploitation are additionally detected, a moderate detection power.
The framework's value lies in its reusability and the FPR control, not in any single detector's absolute performance.

\paragraph{GSM8K cross-benchmark validation.}
To assess portability, we replicate on GSM8K (1,319 items $\times$ 6,702 models).
Temporal DIF yields C$=18.9\%$ (random control: 0.3\%).
Semi-synthetic injection with external-cohort design achieves $\Delta$TPR$=0.82$ at $p=1.0$ and $\Delta$TPR$=0.42$ at $p=0.5$, confirming the framework generalizes to single-domain math benchmarks with higher detection power than the multi-domain MMLU setting.


\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:metacritique}

\paragraph{GSM1K contamination gap correlation.}
Table~\ref{tab:gsm1k} reports the correlation between DIF flags and the GSM8K$-$GSM1K accuracy gap, a proxy for contamination.
The direction is \emph{negative}: items flagged as DIF-C tend to show \emph{smaller} contamination gaps ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$, $N=33$).
The partial correlation controlling for baseline accuracy remains negative ($r=-0.385$, $p=0.027$).
This confirms that DIF and web-overlap-based contamination signals measure different constructs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GSM1K contamination gap vs.\ DIF flag correlation ($N=33$ models). Negative direction indicates DIF and contamination gap are anti-correlated.}
\label{tab:gsm1k}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Metric & Value & CI$_\text{lo}$ & CI$_\text{up}$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
Pearson $r$ & $-$0.478 & $-$0.71 & $-$0.16 & 0.005 \\
Spearman $\rho$ & $-$0.403 & $-$0.67 & $-$0.03 & 0.020 \\
Partial $r$ & $-$0.385 & $-$0.65 & $-$0.04 & 0.027 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{ConStat perturbation comparison.}
We independently evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 220 MMLU items with LLM paraphrasing.
ConStat detects 20.5\% of items (FDR-controlled, FPR$\approx$0.08) with AUROC$=0.74$ (non-RL models).
The overlap between ConStat and DIF flags is low (Jaccard$=0.15$), consistent with the meta-critique: the two methods detect different phenomena.
ConStat identifies memorization via performance drop on paraphrases; DIF identifies differential functioning across temporal cohorts.

\paragraph{DVD generation variance comparison.}
DVD (generation-variance detection) applied to 12 models with 50 responses per item on 1,200 MMLU items yields a bimodal detection rate of 12\% and AUROC$=0.65$.
DVD-DIF overlap is even lower (Jaccard$=0.11$) than ConStat-DIF overlap.
DVD-ConStat overlap (Jaccard$=0.22$) is also modest.
The low pairwise agreement across all three methods strengthens the meta-critique: these detectors are measuring different latent constructs, and validating any of them against web-overlap labels conflates these distinctions.

\paragraph{Ensemble fusion.}
Table~\ref{tab:ensemble} reports the performance of three individual signals and two ensemble strategies.
The weighted ensemble (AUROC$=0.72$) \emph{underperforms} the best individual signal (ConStat, AUROC$=0.74$) by $-0.02$.
Cross-validation weight stability is poor (CV$=0.35$), and the difference between uniform and optimized weighting is negligible (0.03).
This negative result confirms that low inter-method agreement prevents effective signal fusion.
We report this result for transparency; ensemble approaches require higher base-signal agreement to be viable.

