\subsection{Transparency and Diagnostic Analyses}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

\paragraph{Simpson's paradox in DIF predictors.}
Table~\ref{tab:simpsons} reports logistic regression models predicting DIF-C classification.
Model~1 (domain accuracy change only) yields OR$=1.55$ per 0.10 increase: subjects with larger accuracy gains have more DIF items.
Model~2 (adding item difficulty and discrimination) flips the OR to 0.73, a textbook Simpson's paradox.
Item discrimination is the consistent predictor (OR$=1.51$--$1.54$): high-discrimination items are more likely to be flagged as DIF-C.
This is psychometrically expected, since high-discrimination items amplify any ability difference between groups, producing larger MH odds ratio shifts.
Whether this reflects genuine item instability amplified by discrimination, or stable items faithfully discriminating between cohorts of different ability, is the central interpretive challenge.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Simpson's paradox: accuracy change OR flips when controlling for item properties. McFadden $R^2$ is low throughout, indicating DIF classification is driven by factors beyond these predictors.}
\label{tab:simpsons}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Model & Predictors & $R^2_\text{McF}$ & Key OR \\
\midrule
M1 & Subj.\ acc $\Delta$ & 0.0007 & 1.55 \\
M2 & + diff.\ + disc. & 0.0423 & 0.73 \\
M3 & Item acc $\Delta$ & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\
M4 & + diff.\ + disc. & 0.0434 & 0.92 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}
As a robustness check using continuous-covariate DIF \citep{tutz2016item}, Item-Focused Trees (IFT) identify 3,452 DIF items (27.6\%), with 78\% concordance to MH classification.
IFT reveals that 62.3\% of DIF items show \emph{gradual} difficulty drift rather than abrupt change, with median split point at September 2023.
The gradual pattern is consistent with progressive capability development.
MH's binary temporal split captures 78\% of this nuance but necessarily discretizes a continuous process.

