\paragraph{Bifactor IRT equating pilot.}
A bifactor IRT model fitted to the MMLU response matrix (12,508 items $\times$ 5,219 models) fails to converge (iteration limit reached after 18.5 hours).
Fit indices are uniformly poor: RMSEA$=0.089$ ($>0.06$ cutoff), CFI$=0.72$ ($<0.90$ cutoff), TLI$=0.68$, SRMR$=0.11$.
The general factor explains only 28.4\% of variance, confirming that MMLU's 57-subject structure defeats both unidimensional and bifactor IRT specifications.
No anchor items could be selected, and IRT-based test equating is infeasible for MMLU in its current form.

\paragraph{Item generation calibration.}
This experiment was blocked by the bifactor failure: without reliable IRT parameter estimates and anchor items, LLM-generated replacement items cannot be calibrated to an equated scale.
The entire regeneration pipeline (from DIF flag to calibrated replacement) is infeasible under current IRT limitations for multi-domain benchmarks.
This motivates future work on multidimensional IRT or per-subject calibration strategies.


\section{Limitations}

Three primary limitations should inform interpretation of our results.

\emph{IRT model misfit.}
98\% of METABENCH models show $|L_z|>2$ on MMLU, and the bifactor model fails to converge (RMSEA$=0.089$, CFI$=0.72$).
MH-DIF conditions on total score, which is a poor proxy for ability in a 57-subject multidimensional benchmark.
This does not invalidate the statistical signal (permutation $p<0.001$), but it means the matching variable is noisy, potentially inflating DIF rates.
Future work should explore multidimensional IRT or per-subject DIF analysis.

\emph{Causal ambiguity.}
The Simpson's paradox (OR flip from 1.55 to 0.73) and the gradual-drift pattern (62.3\% of DIF items) are consistent with both item instability and capability improvement.
Our semi-synthetic framework validates \emph{detection mechanics}, not \emph{causal interpretation} of detected signals.
Distinguishing genuine item degradation from legitimate ability growth requires experimental designs beyond what observational DIF analysis can provide.

\emph{Sample and scope.}
The GSM1K correlation ($N=33$, CI upper $|r|=0.16$) is suggestive but not definitive.
The reasoning-RL robustness analysis ($N=13$) is underpowered.
Semi-synthetic injection uses a simplified contamination mechanism (incorrect$\to$correct flipping) that may not capture all real-world contamination pathways (e.g., partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts).


\section{Conclusion}

We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework for item-level contamination detectors, with cross-subject matching that achieves zero false-positive inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=0.000$).
The framework is portable across benchmarks (demonstrated on MMLU and GSM8K) and reusable for any item-level detector.
Our empirical meta-critique demonstrates that web-overlap ground truth labels and item-level behavioral signals measure different constructs (enrichment$\approx 1.0$; GSM1K $r=-0.478$), highlighting a systematic validation gap in the contamination detection literature.
As a motivating case study, temporal DIF analysis of 5,227 models across six benchmarks reveals 17.9\%--31.3\% large DIF, with extensive transparency analyses documenting interpretive challenges.

The negative results (bifactor failure, ensemble underperformance, low inter-method agreement) are informative scope boundaries: IRT-based equating requires better model fit than current multi-domain benchmarks provide, and signal fusion requires higher base-signal agreement than current detectors achieve.
We propose that benchmark maintainers adopt DIF-based audit workflows as a diagnostic tool for understanding item-level behavioral changes, while the semi-synthetic framework provides the validation infrastructure that the field currently lacks.


