\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Ranking stability under item replacement.}
Table~\ref{tab:degrade} reports model ranking stability as DIF-flagged items are progressively replaced (by random items, since IRT-equated replacements are unavailable; see \S\ref{sec:negative}).
Even at 50\% replacement, Spearman $\rho=0.988$ and the stability-of-hierarchical-rank coefficient SHR$=0.972$.
MMLU's massive redundancy means DIF-flagged items can be removed without validity loss.
The diagnostic value of DIF lies in understanding \emph{what} changed across generations, not in identifying items that must be removed for quality control.

